/**
@mainpage BCM Simulator - API Reference

An automotive **Body Control Module** built the way a Tier-1 supplier would
build one: strict layering, four state machines behind a central safety
arbiter, a framed diagnostic protocol, and a test strategy that runs most of
the firmware on a PC.

Target: STM32F103C8 "Blue Pill", 72 MHz Cortex-M3, 64 KB flash, 20 KB RAM.

@section arch Layering

@verbatim
  Application   app/          composition root, FreeRTOS tasks
       |
  Service       services/     FSMs, arbitration, protocol, diagnostics
       |
  Driver        drivers/      GPIO, button, LED, UART, ADC, timer, watchdog
       |
  HAL / BSP     bsp/          the only layer that knows the wiring
       |
  STM32 HAL + CMSIS  ->  hardware
@endverbatim

Dependencies point **downward only**. The consequence that matters: every
service compiles for the host, so the control logic is unit-tested and
scenario-tested off-target.

@section split The split that makes testing possible

Each driver is two pieces - hardware-free logic, and a thin binding to it:

| HAL-free (host-tested)   | HAL-bound (target only) |
|--------------------------|-------------------------|
| @ref bcm::drivers::Debouncer  | @ref bcm::drivers::Button |
| @ref bcm::drivers::Blinker    | @ref bcm::drivers::Led |
| @ref bcm::drivers::ExpFilter  | @ref bcm::drivers::Adc |
| @ref bcm::drivers::RingBuffer | @ref bcm::drivers::Uart |
| @ref bcm::drivers::SoftTimer  | @ref bcm::drivers::Watchdog |

The same idea at the top: @ref bcm::services::BcmCore holds the entire control
cycle with no hardware in it. @ref bcm::app::BcmApp binds it to pins; the SIL
harness drives the identical object from a scripted timeline.

@section safety Safety invariants

These are enforced in one place - @ref bcm::services::LampArbiter - which runs
**last**, immediately before anything reaches a pin. No feature and no
diagnostic host can route around it.

- Brake lamp overrides every other lamp (SRS-BRK-001, SRS-SAFETY-003)
- Reverse lamp only with reverse gear selected (SRS-REV-001, SRS-SAFETY-005)
- Never lock while a door is open (SRS-DOOR-002, SRS-SAFETY-004) - an FSM
  guard, so it covers the automatic path as well as the manual one
- Sleep blanks every lamp but the lock indicator (SRS-PWR-003)
- Comms lost drives a defined state, not the last command (SRS-SAFETY-007)
- The watchdog is refreshed only when every task has checked in
  (SRS-SAFETY-006)

@section docs Companion documents

- `docs/requirements/SRS.md` - software requirements (BCM-SRS-001)
- `docs/architecture/ArchitectureDocument.md` - design rationale (BCM-ARC-001)
- `docs/protocol/ICD.md` - the wire protocol (BCM-ICD-001)
- `docs/uml/UML.md` - class, sequence, state and deployment diagrams
- `docs/test/TestReport.md` - results and the requirements traceability matrix
- `docs/manual/UserManual.md` - build, flash, operate, troubleshoot
*/
